## PART 5 — LEGAL PROTECTION AND COMPLIANCE DESIGN

This section governs the quality of all three documents.

The policies must be protective without using legally dangerous language.

### Prohibited drafting practices

Never use blanket declarations such as:

- Botlytices is never responsible for anything.
- All payments are non-refundable in every circumstance.
- Customers waive every legal right.
- Botlytices can never be sued.
- Botlytices accepts no liability under any circumstances.
- Botlytices is not responsible for any loss whatsoever.
- Botlytices can modify any agreement without notice.
- Customers have no right to challenge services.
- Botlytices is not responsible for any privacy or security incident.

Avoid arbitrary clauses that attempt to override mandatory consumer, privacy, data-security or contractual obligations.

Instead, use properly drafted limitations that are proportionate, transparent and legally permitted.

### International law compatibility

Check the applicable requirements for each market.

Use separate country or regional supplements where needed.

Do not force a single standard clause onto all customers regardless of whether they are businesses or consumers.

Distinguish:
- Business-to-business customers.
- Individual consumers.
- Professional clients.
- Subscribers.
- Users of digital content.
- Customers of physical installation services.

Where consumer status affects contractual rights, determine the applicable legal treatment.

